cdot 0+13 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,16) \\\\  & ≡13 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,16)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, note that $-13+13≡0,$ so $x≡-13\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16).$ To write this in the required form, we use the following sequence of congruences:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ≡-13 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,16) \\\\  & ≡0-13 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,16) \\\\  & ≡16-13 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,16) \\\\  & ≡3 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,16)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the additive inverse of the element $45$ modulo $16$ is $3.$\n\nWe can verify this result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}45+3≡48≡3\\cdot 16≡0\\,\\,✓\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** In general, the additive inverse of $a$ modulo $n$ is simply the residue of $-a$ modulo $n,$ i.e. $-a\\,\\text{mod}\\,n.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152492 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152492 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213239" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152492 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152492 #edb/ref 1071 "Expressed as a nonnegative integer smaller than $13,$ the additive inverse of $-50$ modulo $13$ is{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152492 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0337befa-d550-5b7b-a108-eacd390b3246" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152492 #edb/ref 1074 "The additive inverse of $-50$ modulo $13$ is the integer $0\\le x<13$ such that\n\n$$\nx+(-50)≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13).\n$$\n\nFirst, we note that $-50≡2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-50 & ≡(-4)\\cdot 13+2 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\  & ≡(-4)\\cdot 0+2 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\  & ≡2 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, note that $-2+2≡0,$ so $x≡-2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13).$ To write this in the required form, we use the following sequence of congruences:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ≡-2 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\  & ≡0-2 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\  & ≡13-2 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\  & ≡11 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the additive inverse of the element $-50$ modulo $13$ is $11.$\n\nWe can verify this result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-50+11≡-39≡(-3)\\cdot 13≡0\\,\\,✓\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** In general, the additive inverse of $a$ modulo $n$ is simply the residue of $-a$ modulo $n,$ i.e. $-a\\,\\text{mod}\\,n.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152498 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152498 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152498 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213361" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152498 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152498 #edb/ref 1071 "Select the correct options in the reasoning below." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152498 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cc247d20-363d-58cd-8b44-329e84f774bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152498 #edb/ref 1074 "The additive inverse of $12$ modulo $29$ is the integer $0\\le x<29$ such that\n\n$$\nx+12≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,29).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that when we add our two numbers, we get a number that is congruent to zero modulo $29:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}17+12 & ≡29 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,29) \\\\  & ≡0 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,29)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude the following:\n\nThe integer $17$ $\\text{is}$ the additive inverse of $12$ modulo $29$ since $17+12≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,29).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152900 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152900 #edb/ref 1061 133689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152900 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5e2c5392-9766-5e54-8b31-42bf5a899f9a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152900 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153470 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152901 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152901 #edb/ref 1061 133690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152901 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b343338e-8124-5f29-9480-f3367cf8dacd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152901 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152902 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152902 #edb/ref 1061 133693 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152902 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "23aab553-3460-5dca-95ec-e900015ee34a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152902 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152903 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152903 #edb/ref 1061 133696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152903 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "af14900b-79be-585e-bdfb-29d2d41756ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152903 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152064 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152904 #edb/ref 1061 133699 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152904 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ddb3522b-6072-5aac-b170-652be12a8adc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152904 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153470 #edb/ref 1064 "The **additive inverse** of $a$ modulo $n$ is the integer $0 \\leq x \\lt n$ such that\n\n$$\nx + a \\equiv 0 \\quad (\\textrm{mod} \\: n).\n$$\n\nFor example, the integer $7$ is the additive inverse of $5$ modulo $12$ since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7+5≡12≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe can visualize this using a clock diagram.\n\n![](images/5f/5f0c578480e9f9f80820108a0af79ae8eecc826127c6e4d44aa5f3b50a6f086a.png)\n\nOn the other hand, the integer $6$ is *not* the additive inverse of $5$ modulo $12$ since $6 + 5 \\equiv 11\\not\\equiv 0 \\, (\\textrm{mod} \\: 12).$ \n\nIn general, we have the following theorem:\n\n*For every integer, there exists a unique additive inverse for a given modulus $n.$* \n\nNote the following: \n\n- The word \"unique\" in the statement above means with respect to the inequality $0 \\leq x \\lt n.$\n\n- It's important to know that any integer summed with an integer congruent to its inverse will also give zero. \n\nFor example, $x=7$ is the additive inverse of $5$ modulo 